# Yuruna line-ending policy.
#
# Yuruna runs on Windows + Hyper-V hosts AND macOS + UTM hosts, drives
# Linux guest VMs (Ubuntu, Amazon Linux), and ships shell scripts that
# the host status service serves byte-faithfully to those guests over
# HTTP. If a developer's git is configured with core.autocrlf=true (the
# Windows default after some "Git for Windows" installers), `*.sh` files
# are checked out as CRLF, the status service serves CRLF bytes, and the
# guest's bash chokes with `$'\r': command not found` on the very first
# line of fetch-and-execute.sh. This file forces LF on every text type
# a Linux guest reads, regardless of any per-developer config.
#
# Files consumed by Linux guests -- must always be LF.
# The cloud-init seeds are extensionless *suffixes*, not bare names: the
# real files are `<guest>.base.user-data` and `<guest>.meta-data`. A bare
# `user-data` pattern only matches a file named exactly that, so it leaves
# the real seeds unattributed -- and under core.autocrlf=true they check out
# CRLF, which rides through the verbatim WriteAllText in
# Yuruna.CloudInitTemplate.psm1 into the seed ISO and lands as `$'\r':
# command not found` in the guest's runcmd. Match on the suffix.
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.bash          text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
*.service       text eol=lf
*.conf          text eol=lf
*.toml          text eol=lf
*.user-data     text eol=lf
*.meta-data     text eol=lf
*.network-config text eol=lf
user-data       text eol=lf
meta-data       text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
# `.template` hides the real type from every rule above: the files are
# `<name>.yml.template`, `.json.template` and `config.plist.template`, so a
# `*.yml` rule never sees them. They are read verbatim and written into the
# generated config a macOS/Linux consumer then parses, so a CRLF checkout is
# copied straight through into a file that is supposed to be LF.
*.template      text eol=lf

# Go sources for the extension servers (stash-service, pool-control-service,
# pool-aggregator-service, caching-proxy-parser-service). The compiler accepts either EOL, but
# a backtick raw string literal preserves every byte between the quotes -- so
# on a CRLF checkout the CR rides into whatever the literal holds: the
# self-contained HTML pages these servers serve, the SQLite schema DDL, and the
# /proc/self/mountinfo fixtures that tests split line by line. gofmt also emits
# LF unconditionally, so a CRLF tree makes `gofmt -l` report every file as
# unformatted. Pin LF so the source is identical on the Windows and macOS
# workstations and on the Linux VM that builds these binaries.
*.go         text eol=lf
go.mod       text eol=lf
go.sum       text eol=lf

# PowerShell scripts run on both Windows and macOS hosts. pwsh handles
# either EOL, but LF keeps the repo consistent and avoids round-trip
# CRLF<->LF churn when a file is edited on macOS, committed, then re-
# checked-out on Windows.
*.ps1        text eol=lf
*.psm1       text eol=lf
*.psd1       text eol=lf

# OpenTofu/Terraform sources. A heredoc (`<<EOT`) preserves every byte
# between its markers, so on a CRLF checkout the CR rides into whatever the
# literal produces -- cloud-init user-data, a remote-exec provisioner body, a
# config file the Linux node then parses -- and surfaces there as
# `$'\r': command not found` rather than anywhere near the .tf file.
*.tf         text eol=lf
*.tfvars     text eol=lf
*.hcl        text eol=lf

# XML seeds. autounattend.xml is copied verbatim into the Windows guest's
# seed ISO; the public key is read back through RSA FromXmlString. Both parse
# either EOL, so pin the one that keeps a macOS and a Windows checkout
# byte-identical. Must stay ABOVE the *.ps1xml rule below -- attributes are
# last-match-wins, and .ps1xml is also an .xml.
*.xml        text eol=lf

# The release manifest is signed BYTE-FOR-BYTE: install.sha256.sig is a
# detached signature over install.sha256's exact bytes, and a fresh host
# verifies the file it just downloaded. A single CRLF copy committed here
# changes those bytes and every verification fails with "SIGNATURE INVALID
# -- do not run", with nothing to point at the line endings. The keys are
# parsed by openssl (macOS/Linux) and by .NET on Windows.
*.sha256     text eol=lf
*.pem        text eol=lf
*.asc        text eol=lf

# Read verbatim and used as a git ref -- the release tag to check out. A
# trailing CR makes the ref simply not exist.
VERSION      text eol=lf

# Installed into .git/hooks and run by git on whatever platform the developer
# is on. `#!/bin/sh` with a CR is `$'\r': command not found` on the first line.
tools/githooks/*  text eol=lf

# Marker files that keep an otherwise-empty directory in the tree.
global/components/placeholder  text eol=lf
global/workloads/placeholder   text eol=lf

# Windows-only text formats -- must be CRLF.
*.bat        text eol=crlf
*.cmd        text eol=crlf
*.ps1xml     text eol=crlf

# Repo metadata / docs -- LF is fine on every editor we ship for.
*.md         text eol=lf
*.json       text eol=lf
*.jsonc      text eol=lf
*.txt        text eol=lf
*.html       text eol=lf
*.css        text eol=lf
*.js         text eol=lf
.gitignore       text eol=lf
.gitattributes   text eol=lf
.gitconfig.yuruna text eol=lf

# Binaries -- never normalize. Without these, git ships VHDX/ISO bytes
# through CRLF rewriting if it ever misclassifies them as text.
*.png        binary
*.jpg        binary
*.jpeg       binary
*.gif        binary
*.ico        binary
*.iso        binary
*.img        binary
*.vhd        binary
*.vhdx       binary
*.qcow2      binary
*.raw        binary
*.utm        binary
*.pfx        binary
*.zip        binary
*.7z         binary
*.tar        binary
*.tar.gz     binary
*.tgz        binary
*.tar.xz     binary
*.tar.bz2    binary
*.exe        binary
*.dll        binary
# Detached release signature (raw RSA bytes). EOL normalization would corrupt
# it and break verification of install/install.sha256 on a fresh host.
*.sig        binary
